A||N||11||Haze_Performance||Is there any fiberglass guys out there?||||10||
Z||000000||Haze_Performance||11-20-2000||11:05 PM||||I was wondering has anyone ever thought of doing the front end grills (like in the pic)to the 84-86 coupe nose?? (Make deep grills out of the black bumper pads)<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/nov19^34.jpg"> <P><BR>Can someone make one in photoshop?? I would like to see how it would look. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/goodlogo1_1.gif">   <BR><B>"AHHHHH!!! All Fiero's don't start on fire you BONEHEAD!!"</B>||||reg||10
Z||000001||sqoach||11-20-2000||11:13 PM||||speaking of fiberglass, does any1 know if it is possible to make a spoiler?  first shaping it out of some kind of wood, then shooting it with fiberglass???  i've seen many custom spoilers and wondered how they did it.  i guess if i can't figure any thing out i can always go with the original wing.<P>haze, that is a weird car, what is it??<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh||||reg||5
Z||000002||Haze_Performance||11-20-2000||11:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sqoach:<BR><B>haze, that is a weird car, what is it??<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/004448.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/004448.html</A> <P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/goodlogo1_1.gif">   <BR><B>"AHHHHH!!! All Fiero's don't start on fire you BONEHEAD!!"</B>||||reg||14
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||11-20-2000||11:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sqoach:<BR><B>speaking of fiberglass, does any1 know if it is possible to make a spoiler?  first shaping it out of some kind of wood, then shooting it with fiberglass???  i've seen many custom spoilers and wondered how they did it.  i guess if i can't figure any thing out i can always go with the original wing.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'd recommend making a wood frame, and then gluing foam insulation on top of it. (the big blue sheets) Cut and sand this down until you get the right shape, and then cover it with some bondo, and sand again until it's smooth..<BR>test fit it, to make sure it's the right size etc.. then you can make a fiberglass copy (or plaster!) and then shoot a fiberglass version using the mold. <P>Or, if you're really good with making stuff like this.. use a metal frame (1/2" copper pipe works well, plus the available fittings make it easy) instead of the wood, and directly put the fiberglass on top of the foam. This would end up being a very light and strong part.. in fact I might try that myself! a friend who's buying an 87 GT wants to do an Acura NSX-type wing on his car, and I said I'd help him try to do this.. and yes, I know it'll need some mods to the decklid.. I'm not even sure it can be done.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000004||Haze_Performance||11-21-2000||01:25 PM||||Back on top.<P><BR>Anyone??<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/goodlogo1_1.gif">   <BR><B>"AHHHHH!!! All Fiero's don't start on fire you BONEHEAD!!"</B>||||reg||10
Z||000005||rockcrawl||11-21-2000||04:21 PM||||You guys have the right idea. The original part that is used to make the mold is called a plug. It is usually made from rigid foam or wood, and covered with fiberglass composite. The blue (or pink) polystyrene foam insulation will not work with polyester fiberglass resin, it will melt. I'd recommend using polyester foam over wood. The wood provides support and the foam is easily shaped and contoured. If only one finished part is desired, the plug can be used as the finished part, or a mold can be made from it and several identical parts can be made from the mold. <BR> The front fascia is too flexible to do any major fiberglass mods to. To make a custom fascia, a mold would need to be made from an original. A plug would need to be made from the mold and modified to the desired shape, and them a new mold made. It requires a lot of time, but in the case of turning the bumper pads into grilles, maybe they could just be cut out of the original urethane fascia and screens attached from behind. But remember there is a steel bumper behind the fascia, and I'm not sure it would look good exposed.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Pontiaddict||11-21-2000||05:38 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.tir.com/~sjb/festival98/ppg2-98.JPG" TARGET=_blank>Like this?</A> <P><BR>It looks like it has a Pisa Fastback so the front might be made by them also.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Pontiaddict (edited 11-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||11-21-2000||06:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>The blue (or pink) polystyrene foam insulation will not work with polyester fiberglass resin, it will melt. I'd recommend using polyester foam over wood. The wood provides support and the foam is easily shaped and contoured</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What exactly is polyester foam, and how is this different than the sheets of foam insulation? Another question, without using a mold.. how could you get a gelcoat-type finish?<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000008||rogergarrison||11-21-2000||07:35 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR>[B] <A HREF="http://www.tir.com/~sjb/festival98/ppg2-98.JPG" TARGET=_blank>Like this?</A> <BR>ive made custom body parts out of styrofoam carved to shape, covered them with glass matting till it was substantially thick enough, then sprayed laq thinner on it after it cured to desolve the styrofoam. then just finish it out with body filler. On some spoilers i bonded some wood strips inside 1/2" X   1 1/2" for extra strenth. They do this in aftermarket running boards for trucks too||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||11-21-2000||08:21 PM||||If you are talking about altering the facia itself then the other issue is bonding the facia to the new part. That could be very unplesant depending on just what you are doing. I'd try this on scrap parts to make sure it won't fall apart in 5 miles.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||11-21-2000||09:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>What exactly is polyester foam, and how is this different than the sheets of foam insulation? Another question, without using a mold.. how could you get a gelcoat-type finish?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry, I meant to say Polyurethane foam, not polyester. Polyurethane foam is the standard foam core material for fiberglass comosite parts. The foam insulation that you find in sheets at the hardware store is polystyrene (styrofoam) or sometimes a close variation. It differs chemically from polyurethane, and is not at all compatible with polyester resin (trust me, I've tried!). Polyurethane is also a bit more rigid than poltstyrene, and it doesn't melt when shaped with a belt sander like polystrene does. Polyurethane foam can be purchased in sheets in various sizes and thicknesses from any fiberglass supplier.<P>And for your second question. After the foam/wood core is fully reinforced with fiberglass and resin, it can be finished in the same way you would finish a car body. Sand, fill, smooth, prime, paint. I usually add a heavy final layer of gelcoat to the part before final finishing, and if it is a small part that is just going to be used once to make a female mold, I don't even use fiberglass, just a few layers of resin on top of the foam. <P><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||rockcrawl||11-21-2000||10:01 PM||||For anyone who wants to learn more about working with fiberglass, click on <A HREF="http://www.fibreglast.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fibreglast.com/</A>  and then check out the info center. Their prices are way too high, but the site has a lot of good free info.||||reg||1
